<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Junkers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Junkers was a German aircraft and engine manufacturer based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, founded by the engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-junkers">Hugo Junkers</a>. It built early all-metal aircraft and the Ju 52 transport, and after its takeover by the Nazi state in 1935 produced warplanes such as the Ju 87 Stuka and Ju 88 bomber. After 1945 its works in Dessau were dismantled by the Soviet occupiers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/junkers.jpg" alt="Junkers" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-R14718,_Dessau,_Junkers-Werke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Junkers Flugzeug- und Motorenwerke AG, more commonly Junkers, was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1895 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-junkers">Hugo Junkers</a>, and at first made boilers and radiators. All Junkers diesel engines were two-stroke, opposed-piston designs, an arrangement Hugo Junkers invented in the early 1890s as an alternative to Nicholaus Otto's patented four-stroke engine, since it would run on low-grade fuels such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blast-furnace">blast furnace</a> waste gases. By 1896 his engines were generating electrical power in steelworks. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and after it the company became known for pioneering all-metal aircraft, beginning with the Junkers J 1 of 1915, the first full metal-structure aircraft.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The F 13, first flown on 25 June 1919, was the first all-metal airliner. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> initially forbade aircraft construction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and then allowed only civil designs. Junkers partly relocated to Fili, a western suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and restarted aircraft manufacture in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1922. With the treaty restrictions ending in 1926, the W 33 and W 34 series sold widely and set records for duration, distance and altitude, and the four-engined G 38 was the largest landplane in the world when it entered service with Deutsche Luft Hansa. William Bushnell Stout in 1922 and Andrei Tupolev in 1924 adapted Junkers's corrugated duralumin airframe technology.</p><p>Around 1931 financial difficulties led to the collapse of the group of companies, and the shareholders pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-junkers">Hugo Junkers</a> to leave. A plan to buy his engine patents into a new company, Junkers Motoren-Patentstelle GmbH, was formed in November 1932, but the collapse was solved instead by selling Junkers Thermo Technik GmbH to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-bosch-gmbh">Robert Bosch GmbH</a>. Hugo Junkers was removed by the Nazis in 1934, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the company produced planes for the German air force and piston and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jet-engine">jet engines</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The company survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, despite heavy bomb damage, and the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>, and was reconstituted as Junkers GmbH. It merged into the MBB consortium through the Flugzeug-Union-Süd joint venture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinkel">Heinkel</a> and Messerschmitt in 1958. Messerschmitt took control of the company in 1965 and absorbed it in 1967. In the 1950s and early 1960s Junkers GmbH did research on future aerospace transport, and in 1961 the Austrian engineer Eugen Sänger completed a design for an advanced orbital spacecraft there. The Junkers name disappeared in 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Junkers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many <b>Junkers</b> opposed Hohenzollern rule from Berlin, and most of society kept the tie with Poland as a safeguard against absolutism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>East of the Elbe, <b>Junker</b> landlords held estates worked by dependent peasants under the manorial system known as Gutsherrschaft, and the nobility supplied the officer corps, while townspeople paid the excise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the fifteenth century the nobility, the <b>Junkers</b>, turned their estates into large grain-producing manors worked by peasants bound by heavy labour services.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugo Junkers founded his first works at Dessau in 1895, later the base of the <b>Junkers</b> aircraft factory.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a></li><li><blockquote>Its industries included the <b>Junkers</b> aircraft works and chemical plants at Bitterfeld nearby.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-anhalt">Free State of Anhalt</a></li></ul></section>
